  "notes": "A Danish hip-hop-DJ-plus-jazz-keyboardist duo making big-ballad R&B over a hip-hop floor, 1994–2010. The band is \"60–100; ballads 60–85 half-time\", and the ballad half is the base with `halfTime` true and the mode at 67, a sourced multi-vocalist record. The three drum deltas come from the entry's own phrase — \"hip-hop kit under a ballad\": a sampled, boom-bap-flavoured kick at one to three hits a bar with `quantizeStrength` at 0.66 and a snare pushed 3–8 ms off the grid, a clap layered 2–6 ms behind that snare on beat 3, and \"16th hats low\" as a continuous lane at 0.22 fill density. Their stated identity — \"our different backgrounds are what make our tracks interesting\" — is exactly this split: a DJ's floor under a jazz keyboardist's harmony. That harmony is the identifying parameter: \"keyboard-led 4-chord cycles with 7ths/9ths and real cadences (Karlin's jazz training), designed for multi-vocalist arrangements\", so `ii–v–i` shares top weight with the four-chord minor family, the triad share falls to 0.16, and the melody is written wide (MIDI 55–86) because \"their signature records are duets and trios where the harmony has to support three leads\". The counter is \"guitar or string counterline\" at −12. The bass is \"sub-kick reinforcement plus a played or synth bass on roots; the low end is warm rather than 808-forward\", so no `bass808` block. ⚠ Confidence is medium: the research says \"harmonic detail partly inferred from the stated jazz-keyboard identity\". ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED.",
